THE LAW OF INNOCENCE by Michael Connelly

2020. Allen&Unwin. Paperback.

This was a re-read of a Mickey Haller ("Lincoln Lawyer") thriller and
I'd forgotten all of the plot details so it was just as good as a
first read. Harry Bosch is in this story as well to help Haller's
defence team, but this is really a Lincoln Lawyer story and I find I
enjoy the Mickey Haller stories more; probably because the Bosch
stories have such gruesome crimes.

Michael Connelly has a nice habit of dropping real-world events into
his thrillers and during the course of The Law of Innocence, Covid-19
starts to spread.

The Law of Innocence follows on from events in The Fifth Witness which
I'll re-read later this year and consider as a "prequel" to this
book. I'm pleased to say that the re-readability of Connelly books so
far has proven very high; which is unusuaul from crime stories.

There wasn't as much Bosch in the book as I would've liked; indeed the
final twists of the book come from information supplied by the FBI not
from the homicide detective. The plot device of the Mafia running
scams on the government to take biofuels subsidies by running the same
trucks in and out of refineries with no processing taking place I
found very satisfying.

